Z||000008||DRH||09-15-2001||01:32 AM||||Make no mistake, ground troops WILL be involved. They need to be in place BEFORE we start shooting missles and bombing and the enemy starts scattering (any worse than they already are).<P>Think of it like this... These guys are a bunch of cockroaches. We want to make sure we don't flip on the light until we have enough boots ready to stomp ALL of them.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||deceler8||09-15-2001||11:42 AM||||I believe there is lots of quiet for a reason...it isn't only us that is positioning forces and planning operations...I am assuming the Russians have some surprises for Mr. bin Laden and the Afghans in the offing.<P>It does take time for ships and support forces to be properly positioned to provide a sustained attack. Our response will not be like Clinton's, where a handful of cruise missiles were targeted at what turned out to be empty bases. There is an advanced intelligence actively searching for the coordinates of whatever parties are responsible, and when sufficient information is obtained, and adequate force is assembled, we will strike.<P>And the absolute destruction inflicted in these strikes will be shocking. <P>If I were Mr. bin Laden, I would pray the Americans arrive before the Russians. But I suspect Mr. bin Laden is already dead, in a scene reminiscient of the end of Hitler's Germany...dead of self-inflicted wounds in some bunker.<P>In any event, I would capture the Taliban leaders and put them on trial in America...and when found guilty, publicly hanged on the site of the World Trade Towers.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cliff Pennock||09-15-2001||12:18 PM||||<I>"I wonder how long we would wait if this was Russia that crashed planes into new york and washington."</I><P>That's the problem right there. In such a case there would be a clear cut enemy to fight. It's hard to go to war if your enemy is unknown. Of course Bin Laden is suspect number one, but it would be political suicide if you'd go in and you are attacking the wrong enemy. You need to be absolutely sure you are crippeling the one that needs to be crippled, otherwise you are opening the door to new terrorist actions. You don't want to start a <B>Jihad</B> (holy war) while not eliminating the possibility of a repeat of what happened the 11th.<P>Anyway, my 2c.||||reg||1
Z||000011||DRH||09-15-2001||02:25 PM||||Just thought of something else...<P>- It's a good bet that ALL of these guys in the US (and allied countries) didn't die Tuesday.<P>- It's also a good bet that some sort of counter attack to answer OUR response has already been planned.<P>It would be a <B>really</B> bad idea to set this thing in motion until our intelligence has a really good idea who these people are and what they might have planned. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Urchin||09-15-2001||02:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DRH:<BR><B>Just thought of something else...<P>- It's a good bet that ALL of these guys in the US (and allied countries) didn't die Tuesday.<P>- It's also a good bet that some sort of counter attack to answer OUR response has already been planned.<P>It would be a [b]really</B> bad idea to set this thing in motion until our intelligence has a really good idea who these people are and what they might have planned. [/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I totally agree and have actually considered that myself. The only problem is, that there is practically no way they could determine what a counter attack may entail.  Especially when you are dealing with people of this sort.  I guess all you can do is take maximum precautions on your own soil and hope for the best.  Of course, the Taliban has vowed revenge, so that may mean that they wait until this has blown over (however long that may take), and strike (probably another terrorist act) just when you are least suspecting it.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/final.jpg"> <BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||1
